{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The same build works in staging and breaks in production</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team builds their frontend once in CI, expecting to deploy that exact built artifact to staging, verify it, then promote the identical files to production. With Vite or webpack, if the API URL is set through an environment variable read at build time, <code>import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL</code> or <code>process.env.API_URL</code> through webpack's DefinePlugin, that value gets baked into the JavaScript bundle as a literal string the moment the build runs. The "staging" build and the "production" build are actually two different sets of files, because the env var was different each time the build command ran, even though the source code never changed.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      This breaks the "build once, promote everywhere" model most teams assume they have. If a build was made with <code>VITE_API_URL=https://staging.api.example.com</code>, that URL is now a string literal sitting inside the minified bundle, there's no runtime lookup happening. Redeploying the exact same files to production still points at staging. The only way to get a production-pointing bundle is to run the build again with different env vars, producing a different artifact, one that technically never passed the staging verification the team thought they were relying on.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The root cause is that <code>import.meta.env</code> and DefinePlugin are text-substitution tools by design, not runtime configuration systems. They exist so a minifier can dead-code-eliminate environment-specific branches, <code>if (import.meta.env.PROD) { ... }</code>, which requires the value to already be a literal at build time. Using them for config that legitimately needs to vary per deploy target, API URLs, flag-service endpoints, analytics keys, is applying a compile-time tool to a runtime problem. The tool works exactly as documented; the mismatch is in what it's being asked to do.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Fetch config at boot instead of baking it in at build</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The fix is a small JSON file, say <code>/config.json</code>, served alongside the built app and fetched once when the app starts. It holds the environment-specific values, <code>{"apiUrl": "https://api.example.com"}</code>. The exact same built JS bundle runs in every environment; only <code>config.json</code> differs per deployment, and it can be swapped without touching or rebuilding the app's code at all.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A close variant is window-injected config: the HTML shell, which unlike the JS bundle usually is regenerated or templated per deploy, sets a global before the app's script tag runs, <code>&lt;script&gt;window.__APP_CONFIG__ = {"apiUrl": "..."}&lt;/script&gt;</code>, and the app reads from <code>window.__APP_CONFIG__</code> instead of <code>import.meta.env</code>. Either approach moves the "which environment am I in" question from build time to boot time, which is exactly the point at which the answer is actually knowable.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This gets teams the property they usually want without realizing they're asking for it: a single built artifact that's identical byte-for-byte across every environment, so what got verified in staging is provably the same code running in production, not "code built from the same source at a different time with different substitutions." The cost is one extra network request, or one extra inline script tag, at boot, and the app has to tolerate config not being synchronously available on the very first render, a real, if usually small, engineering cost worth being explicit about rather than papering over.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>Same commit, same build, two different backends</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team's CI pipeline built their frontend once per commit and pushed the resulting <code>dist/</code> folder to a staging bucket for QA sign-off, then, once approved, copied the identical files to the production bucket. It looked correct: same commit, same build, same files. But the build had run with <code>VITE_API_URL</code> pointing at staging, so the "promoted" production files were quietly still calling the staging API. They happened to be reading data close enough to production's that nobody noticed for two days.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The team's mental model was "build once, promote the artifact", a standard and genuinely good practice for backend services with runtime env vars, but their frontend build baked the URL in as a literal string during the build step, so there wasn't actually one artifact being promoted, there were two different builds that happened to share a git commit. The fix wasn't a process change, sign off after building against prod-shaped config too, it was removing the env-var dependency from the build entirely.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      They moved to a <code>config.json</code> fetched at app boot, generated by their deploy step rather than their build step, and placed alongside the already-built, environment-agnostic bundle. The actual <code>dist/</code> output now gets built once and stored in an artifact registry, and the same tarball is deployed to staging and production with a different <code>config.json</code> dropped next to it, which finally matched the mental model the team had assumed they had all along.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

