{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Runtime config has to tolerate not being there yet</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Fetching config.json at boot means there's a brief window before it resolves where the app doesn't know its own API URL yet. The boot sequence has to account for that, a small loading state, or blocking the very first render briefly on that one fast local fetch, rather than assuming config is synchronously available the way a build-time constant always is.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A build-time constant can be referenced anywhere, including inside modules evaluated before the app even mounts. A runtime-fetched config can't, so code that used to read <code>import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL</code> at module scope has to be refactored to read from a config object threaded through app initialization instead, a real one-time migration cost, not just a config-format swap.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      <code>config.json</code> itself needs the opposite caching behavior from the rest of the bundle. The JS and CSS assets should be aggressively, immutably cached, they're content-hashed, but config.json has to be fetched fresh on every boot, <code>Cache-Control: no-cache</code> or a cache-busting query param, otherwise a stale cached config defeats the entire point of decoupling config from the build: a browser or CDN serving a day-old config.json is the same failure mode as a stale build-time constant, just moved one layer over.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

